#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/outcomes/outcomes — the task-outcome log's mechanism (task 0070).

WHAT THIS IS FOR (read before touching it)

`docs/measurement.md` is the authority; this script is its teeth. The
short version of why it exists: interrupted time series is the only
credible causal design available to a project with one resident, and it
is worthless without pre-intervention data
(`docs/research/measurement-methodology.md`, design implication 9). So
`docs/log/task-outcomes.tsv` starts before the pipeline-changing work
lands, and something has to keep it honest afterwards.

THE SPLIT, AND WHY IT MATTERS

Three subcommands, deliberately unequal:

  `derive`   reads git and — when pointed at one — a harness journal, and
             writes rows. It needs a repository and a history. Receipts
             only: it never touches a verdict cell.
  `redirect` transcribes a verdict the resident stated into the row,
             citing where they stated it, and verifies against the forge
             or the journal that the citation resolves and that the
             resident wrote it. It needs the world (task 0072).
  `check`    is a pure function of (log, working tree). No network, no
             forge, no model, no clock. It is what CI runs.

That is the same split `tools/handover-ledger.py` and
`tools/handover-check.py` make, for the same reason: a check that
re-derived truth from the live world would fail for reasons that have
nothing to do with the thing it is checking. Here it buys something
more specific — `check` can run on any clone, forever, including one
made after the forge this repo lives on has stopped existing.

WHAT `check` GUARDS

The log's value is entirely in nobody being able to quietly improve its
past. Every rule below exists to make a rewrite loud:

  * Rows are append-only. A row present in the base revision is present
    here, and its immutable cells are byte-identical.
  * Pending cells are write-once: `-` (not recorded) may become a value
    exactly once, and a value never becomes something else. `note` is
    the single exception, because a note is the only cell that could
    ever need redacting.
  * Every brief in `docs/tasks/` has a row. This is the detector: a task
    that lands without being logged fails the very next pull request,
    which is the only moment the row is still cheap to write.
  * A verdict cell carries a citation, and the count *is* the
    citations. Receipts are machine-derived; verdicts are the
    resident's, and a verdict with no reference to where the resident
    said it is the system grading its own judgment
    (`docs/architecture.md`, Proposal 06). `redirects` equals the number
    of distinct `redirect/` citations in `verdict_ref`, so the number
    cannot be edited without the evidence for it changing too.
  * Citations are append-only, which is how a verdict is allowed to
    mature without being rewritable. The base revision's citation list
    must be a prefix of this one, and a verdict number may differ from
    the base only in a revision that added a citation licensing the
    move. `redirects` 1 -> 2 beside a new citation is a second redirect;
    the same change without one is a rewrite.
  * `redirects_wrong` may sit `-` beside a recorded `redirects`. That is
    *pending*, and it is the truthful value until the resident has
    looked again. The old rule — never log a detection rate without its
    miscorrection rate — now binds at reporting time instead, where the
    alternative was defaulting the cell to 0 and recording the absence
    of a judgment as a judgment of correctness.
  * A probe row says it is a probe. Salt never masquerades as a real
    record — same proposal.
  * `note` is lint against the one hard rule this repository has: no
    personal data, ever.

Stdlib only, like `agent/castle` and `tools/clarify/clarify`, and for
the same reason: this has to keep working on someone else's machine in
ten years with nothing installed but a python3 binary.

USAGE

    tools/outcomes/outcomes check [--log FILE] [--repo-root DIR]
                                  [--base REF] [--no-base]
    tools/outcomes/outcomes derive [--log FILE] [--repo-root DIR]
                                   [--harness-journal FILE]... [--env KEY]
                                   [--fill] [-o FILE]
    tools/outcomes/outcomes redirect TASK --ref CITATION [--wrong N]
                                     [--attempt N] [--resident LOGIN]
                                     [--repo OWNER/NAME] [--journal-dir DIR]

`redirect` has its own long help (`redirect --help`) carrying the line
it must not cross: it transcribes a verdict the resident stated, it
never authors one, and what its mechanical checks cannot see is named
there rather than left to be assumed.

`check` exits non-zero having printed every problem it found. `--base`
takes the revision the log's history is held against: `origin/main` by
default, and in CI the *merge base* with it, so a branch cut before
another row landed is not accused of deleting that row. `derive` prints candidate rows for briefs with no
row; `--fill` instead rewrites the log with pending cells filled from
artifact state, for a human to read as a diff.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import datetime
import json
import os
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys

SCHEMA = "1"

# The columns, in order. Changing this list is a schema version bump:
# add the new version's definition to docs/measurement.md, teach this
# script both, and never rewrite existing rows to the new shape.
COLUMNS = [
    "schema",
    "task",
    "attempt",
    "queued",
    "landed",
    "outcome",
    "tier",
    "model",
    "milestone",
    "pr",
    "cost_usd",
    "turns",
    "questions",
    "question_wait_h",
    "redirects",
    "redirects_wrong",
    "reworks",
    "findings",
    "findings_fixed",
    "verdict_ref",
    "probe",
    "env",
    "provenance",
    "note",
]

# Cells fixed when the row is appended. A change to one of these is a
# rewrite of history, which is the thing the log exists to prevent.
IMMUTABLE = {"schema", "task", "attempt", "queued", "tier", "model",
             "milestone", "probe", "env", "provenance"}

# Cells that may go from `-` to a value exactly once, as the attempt's
# outcome matures: the pull request opens, the review lands.
PENDING = {"landed", "outcome", "pr", "cost_usd", "turns", "questions",
           "question_wait_h", "reworks", "findings", "findings_fixed"}

# The one amendable cell. See the header.
AMENDABLE = {"note"}

# Cells the resident authors. Nothing in this repository may infer one.
VERDICT = {"redirects", "redirects_wrong"}

# The verdict columns and the citations behind them, governed by the
# citation rules below rather than by the flat write-once rule (task
# 0072). A verdict is not one fact written once; it is a running count
# of things the resident said, and it moves only because a new citation
# was recorded. `check_cited_append_only` holds that.
CITED = {"redirects", "redirects_wrong", "verdict_ref"}

UNRECORDED = "-"

# No in-flight value here on purpose: `outcome` is a pending cell and a
# pending cell is written once, so an `open` that later became `merged`
# would be exactly the rewrite this log exists to forbid. `-` carries
# "has not reached a terminal state", and a `-` on a task that plainly
# landed is a visible defect rather than a silent one.
OUTCOMES = {"merged", "abandoned", "superseded"}
TIERS = {"deep", "standard", "cheap", "human"}
PROVENANCE = {"live", "backfilled"}

DATE_RE = re.compile(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$")
TASK_RE = re.compile(r"^\d{4}-[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$")
KEY_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9]+([._/-][a-z0-9]+)*$")
INT_RE = re.compile(r"^\d+$")
MONEY_RE = re.compile(r"^\d+\.\d{2}$")
HOURS_RE = re.compile(r"^\d+\.\d$")

# A citation names its kind and then where the resident said it. The
# kind is in the cell rather than implied by position because one row
# accumulates both: the redirects themselves, and the later reassessment
# of whether they were right. `redirects` is the number of `redirect/`
# citations, so the count and the evidence for it cannot drift apart.
CITATION_KINDS = ("redirect", "reassessed")
CITATION_RE = re.compile(
    r"^(?P<kind>redirect|reassessed)/"
    r"(?P<body>pr-\d+-(?:comment|review)-\d+|record-[0-9A-Za-z][0-9A-Za-z._-]*)$"
)

NOTE_MAX = 200
# Crude, deliberate, and cheap: the two shapes personal data has taken
# in this repository's near misses. Not a substitute for reading the
# diff — a floor under it.
NOTE_BANNED = ("@", "/home/")


class Fail(Exception):
    pass


def read_log(path):
    """Parse a task-outcome log into (header, [dict]). Structural only."""
    text = pathlib.Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    if not text.endswith("\n"):
        raise Fail(f"{path}: no trailing newline")
    lines = text.split("\n")[:-1]
    if not lines:
        raise Fail(f"{path}: empty")
    header = lines[0].split("\t")
    rows = []
    for n, line in enumerate(lines[1:], start=2):
        cells = line.split("\t")
        rows.append({"_line": n, "_cells": cells})
    return header, rows


def as_record(row):
    return dict(zip(COLUMNS, row["_cells"]))


def key(rec):
    return (rec["task"], rec["attempt"])


def parse_citations(cell):
    """`verdict_ref` as [(kind, body, text)], plus what is wrong with it.

    The cell is a comma-separated list because a verdict is a running
    count of events, not one fact: a second redirect adds a second
    citation rather than editing the first. Order is the order they were
    recorded, and it is load-bearing — the base revision's list must be a
    *prefix* of this one, which is how "appended" is told from "edited".
    """
    if cell == UNRECORDED:
        return [], []
    out, problems, seen = [], [], set()
    for part in cell.split(","):
        if part != part.strip() or not part:
            problems.append(f"verdict_ref has an empty or padded citation in {cell!r}")
            continue
        m = CITATION_RE.match(part)
        if not m:
            problems.append(
                f"verdict_ref citation {part!r} is not "
                "<redirect|reassessed>/<pr-N-comment-N|pr-N-review-N|record-ID>"
            )
            continue
        if part in seen:
            problems.append(
                f"verdict_ref cites {part!r} twice; the count is of *distinct* "
                "cited events, so a repeat is a double count"
            )
            continue
        seen.add(part)
        out.append((m.group("kind"), m.group("body"), part))
    return out, problems


def check_cited_append_only(was, now, rel, line, problems):
    """The write-once discipline, held per citation instead of per cell.

    A verdict cell that could never move would make the log unable to
    record a second redirect, and a verdict cell that could be edited
    freely would make the count worthless. The rule that gives both:
    citations are append-only, and a verdict number may differ from the
    base revision's only in a revision that added a citation of the kind
    that licenses it. So `redirects` 1 -> 2 is fine with a new
    `redirect/` citation beside it and is a rewrite without one, and a
    bare edit of the digit is always a rewrite.
    """
    old_cites, _ = parse_citations(was["verdict_ref"])
    new_cites, _ = parse_citations(now["verdict_ref"])
    old_keys = [c[2] for c in old_cites]
    new_keys = [c[2] for c in new_cites]
    if new_keys[: len(old_keys)] != old_keys:
        problems.append(
            f"{rel}:{line}: verdict_ref was {was['verdict_ref']!r} at the base and "
            f"is {now['verdict_ref']!r} here; citations are appended, never "
            "edited, removed or reordered"
        )
        return
    added = new_keys[len(old_keys):]
    for name, kind in (("redirects", "redirect"), ("redirects_wrong", "reassessed")):
        if was[name] == now[name]:
            continue
        if was[name] == UNRECORDED:
            # Pending becoming a value for the first time is the ordinary
            # maturation every other pending cell gets, and it needs no
            # licence here because `check_values` has already pinned the
            # number to the citations standing beside it. Without this a
            # row could never record `redirects` 0 — "no redirect
            # happened" is a real thing to say and it cites nothing.
            continue
        if not any(k.startswith(kind + "/") for k in added):
            problems.append(
                f"{rel}:{line}: {name} was {was[name]!r} at the base and is "
                f"{now[name]!r} here, with no new {kind} citation to license it; "
                "a verdict moves only because the resident said something new"
            )


# --- check ---------------------------------------------------------------


def check_shape(header, rows, path, problems):
    if header != COLUMNS:
        problems.append(
            f"{path}:1: header is not schema {SCHEMA}'s columns; "
            f"expected {len(COLUMNS)} names in the order docs/measurement.md gives"
        )
        return False
    ok = True
    for row in rows:
        cells = row["_cells"]
        if len(cells) != len(COLUMNS):
            problems.append(
                f"{path}:{row['_line']}: {len(cells)} cells, expected {len(COLUMNS)}"
            )
            ok = False
            continue
        for name, cell in zip(COLUMNS, cells):
            if cell == "":
                problems.append(
                    f"{path}:{row['_line']}: {name} is empty; "
                    f"not recorded is `{UNRECORDED}`, never blank"
                )
                ok = False
            elif cell != cell.strip():
                problems.append(f"{path}:{row['_line']}: {name} has edge whitespace")
                ok = False
    return ok


def check_values(rows, path, problems):
    def bad(row, msg):
        problems.append(f"{path}:{row['_line']}: {msg}")

    def num(rec, name, pattern):
        v = rec[name]
        return v == UNRECORDED or bool(pattern.match(v))

    seen = {}
    for row in rows:
        rec = as_record(row)
        if rec["schema"] != SCHEMA:
            bad(row, f"schema {rec['schema']!r} is not {SCHEMA!r}")
        if not TASK_RE.match(rec["task"]):
            bad(row, f"task {rec['task']!r} is not NNNN-slug")
        if not INT_RE.match(rec["attempt"]) or rec["attempt"] == "0":
            bad(row, f"attempt {rec['attempt']!r} is not a positive integer")
        k = key(rec)
        if k in seen:
            bad(row, f"{k[0]} attempt {k[1]} already has a row at line {seen[k]}")
        seen[k] = row["_line"]
        for name in ("queued", "landed"):
            if rec[name] == UNRECORDED:
                continue
            if not DATE_RE.match(rec[name]):
                bad(row, f"{name} {rec[name]!r} is not YYYY-MM-DD")
            else:
                # Shape is not a calendar: DATE_RE admits 2026-13-40.
                # Parse it so an impossible date is a defect, not a
                # silently-stored one. Task 0070 review, finding P2.
                try:
                    datetime.date.fromisoformat(rec[name])
                except ValueError:
                    bad(row, f"{name} {rec[name]!r} is not a real date")
        if UNRECORDED not in (rec["queued"], rec["landed"]):
            if rec["landed"] < rec["queued"]:
                bad(row, f"landed {rec['landed']} precedes queued {rec['queued']}")
        if rec["outcome"] != UNRECORDED and rec["outcome"] not in OUTCOMES:
            bad(row, f"outcome {rec['outcome']!r} is not one of {sorted(OUTCOMES)}")
        if rec["outcome"] == "merged" and rec["landed"] == UNRECORDED:
            bad(row, "outcome is merged with no landed date")
        if rec["tier"] != UNRECORDED and rec["tier"] not in TIERS:
            bad(row, f"tier {rec['tier']!r} is not one of {sorted(TIERS)}")
        if rec["provenance"] not in PROVENANCE:
            bad(row, f"provenance {rec['provenance']!r} is not one of {sorted(PROVENANCE)}")
        for name in ("pr", "turns", "questions", "findings", "findings_fixed",
                     "redirects", "redirects_wrong"):
            if not num(rec, name, INT_RE):
                bad(row, f"{name} {rec[name]!r} is not an integer")
        if not num(rec, "cost_usd", MONEY_RE):
            bad(row, f"cost_usd {rec['cost_usd']!r} is not a 2-decimal amount")
        if not num(rec, "question_wait_h", HOURS_RE):
            bad(row, f"question_wait_h {rec['question_wait_h']!r} is not a 1-decimal count of hours")
        for name in ("model", "milestone", "env", "probe", "reworks"):
            if rec[name] != UNRECORDED and not KEY_RE.match(rec[name].replace(",", "-")):
                bad(row, f"{name} {rec[name]!r} is not a bare key")
        # Proposal 06: only verdicts settle, and a verdict is something
        # the resident said somewhere findable. Schema 1 spells that out
        # as kinded citations, one per event (task 0072).
        cites, cite_problems = parse_citations(rec["verdict_ref"])
        for msg in cite_problems:
            bad(row, msg)
        n_redirect = sum(1 for c in cites if c[0] == "redirect")
        n_reassessed = sum(1 for c in cites if c[0] == "reassessed")

        # `redirects` counts distinct cited redirect events, so the count
        # and its citations are one fact written twice and must agree.
        # This is what makes the number unforgeable rather than merely
        # discouraged: an edit of the digit alone is caught here, and the
        # digit can only rise because a citation was appended, which is
        # the one verdict_ref change `check_cited_append_only` permits.
        if rec["redirects"] == UNRECORDED:
            if n_redirect:
                bad(row, f"verdict_ref cites {n_redirect} redirect event(s) and "
                         "redirects is unrecorded")
        elif INT_RE.match(rec["redirects"]) and int(rec["redirects"]) != n_redirect:
            bad(row, f"redirects is {rec['redirects']} and verdict_ref cites "
                     f"{n_redirect} redirect event(s); the count is the citations")

        # `redirects_wrong` is pending until the resident reassesses, and
        # pending is `-`. Writing 0 at redirect time would record the
        # absence of a judgment as a judgment of correctness and bias the
        # conditional miscorrection rate downward — the defect this
        # project keeps paying for, in the one column where it would
        # corrupt a published measure. So `-` beside a recorded
        # `redirects` is legal here and means "not yet reassessed". The
        # pairing rule this replaces now binds where it belongs, at
        # reporting time: docs/measurement.md forbids publishing a
        # detection rate without saying how much of it is unreassessed.
        if rec["redirects_wrong"] != UNRECORDED:
            if rec["redirects"] == UNRECORDED:
                bad(row, "redirects_wrong recorded without redirects")
            elif INT_RE.match(rec["redirects_wrong"]) and INT_RE.match(rec["redirects"]):
                if int(rec["redirects_wrong"]) > int(rec["redirects"]):
                    bad(row, "redirects_wrong exceeds redirects")
                # Reassessing zero redirects is arithmetic, not judgment,
                # so it needs no citation. Anything else does.
                elif int(rec["redirects"]) > 0 and not n_reassessed:
                    bad(row, "redirects_wrong is a verdict and verdict_ref cites no "
                             "resident reassessment; a value here says the resident "
                             "looked again, so say where")
        if UNRECORDED not in (rec["findings"], rec["findings_fixed"]):
            if int(rec["findings_fixed"]) > int(rec["findings"]):
                bad(row, "findings_fixed exceeds findings")
        note = rec["note"]
        if note != UNRECORDED:
            if len(note) > NOTE_MAX:
                bad(row, f"note is {len(note)} characters, over the {NOTE_MAX} cap")
            for banned in NOTE_BANNED:
                if banned in note:
                    bad(row, f"note contains {banned!r}; "
                             "the log carries no personal data, ever")
            if not note.isprintable():
                bad(row, "note has non-printable characters")


def briefs(repo_root):
    """Every numbered brief, by stem, wherever it currently sits."""
    root = pathlib.Path(repo_root) / "docs" / "tasks"
    found = {}
    for d in (root, root / "done"):
        if not d.is_dir():
            continue
        for f in sorted(d.glob("[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-*.md")):
            found[f.stem] = f
    return found


def resolve(task, have, by_number):
    """The brief a row's `task` cell names, allowing for a renamed slug.

    A row's `task` is immutable and a row is never deleted, so a brief
    renamed after its row was written — 0067's brief and branch already
    disagree about a slug — would otherwise deadlock the log: coverage
    would demand a row that append-only forbids writing. The rename is
    resolved by number, but only where the number identifies exactly one
    brief. Two briefs share the number 0003 in this repository's
    history, and guessing which of them a stale stem meant is precisely
    the kind of invention the log exists to keep out.
    """
    if task in have:
        return task
    stems = by_number.get(task[:4], [])
    if len(stems) == 1:
        return stems[0]
    return None


def check_coverage(rows, repo_root, path, problems):
    """The detector: a brief that landed without a row fails the next PR."""
    have = briefs(repo_root)
    by_number = {}
    for stem in have:
        by_number.setdefault(stem[:4], []).append(stem)

    covered = set()
    for row in rows:
        rec = as_record(row)
        if rec["probe"] != UNRECORDED:
            # A probe row names a synthetic task on purpose, and says so.
            if rec["task"] in have:
                problems.append(
                    f"{path}:{row['_line']}: probe row names the real brief "
                    f"{rec['task']}; salt never masquerades as a real record"
                )
            continue
        stem = resolve(rec["task"], have, by_number)
        if stem is None:
            problems.append(
                f"{path}:{row['_line']}: task {rec['task']} matches no brief under "
                "docs/tasks/ or docs/tasks/done/"
            )
        else:
            covered.add(stem)

    for stem in sorted(have):
        if stem not in covered:
            problems.append(
                f"{have[stem]}: brief has no row in {path}; "
                "append one in this pull request — see docs/measurement.md"
            )


def git_show(repo_root, ref, relpath):
    """The file's content at `ref`, or None if the file is not there yet.

    The two failures are told apart on purpose. A ref that does not
    resolve is an error: a typo in `--base`, or a CI checkout with no
    trunk fetched, would otherwise skip the append-only comparison
    entirely and report `ok` — a silently absent guard on the one thing
    this whole design exists to guard. Use --no-base to skip it on
    purpose.
    """
    resolved = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{ref}^{{commit}}"],
        cwd=repo_root, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    if resolved.returncode != 0:
        raise Fail(
            f"{ref} does not resolve to a commit, so the log's history "
            "cannot be checked; pass --base with a reachable revision, or "
            "--no-base to skip the comparison deliberately"
        )
    proc = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "show", f"{ref}:{relpath}"],
        cwd=repo_root, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return proc.stdout


def check_append_only(rows, repo_root, log_path, base, problems):
    """Compare against the base revision. This is the load-bearing check."""
    try:
        rel = str(pathlib.Path(log_path).resolve().relative_to(
            pathlib.Path(repo_root).resolve()))
    except ValueError:
        print(f"note: {log_path} is outside {repo_root}; "
              "skipping the append-only comparison", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    text = git_show(repo_root, base, rel)
    if text is None:
        print(f"note: {rel} does not exist at {base}; "
              "treating every row as new", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    base_lines = text.split("\n")[:-1]
    if not base_lines:
        return
    base_rows = {}
    for line in base_lines[1:]:
        cells = line.split("\t")
        if len(cells) != len(COLUMNS):
            continue
        rec = dict(zip(COLUMNS, cells))
        base_rows[key(rec)] = rec
    here = {}
    for row in rows:
        rec = as_record(row)
        here[key(rec)] = (row["_line"], rec)
    for k, was in sorted(base_rows.items()):
        if k not in here:
            problems.append(
                f"{rel}: the row for {k[0]} attempt {k[1]} is present at {base} "
                "and gone here; rows are never deleted"
            )
            continue
        line, now = here[k]
        check_cited_append_only(was, now, rel, line, problems)
        for name in COLUMNS:
            if name in AMENDABLE or name in CITED:
                continue
            if was[name] == now[name]:
                continue
            if name in IMMUTABLE:
                problems.append(
                    f"{rel}:{line}: {name} was {was[name]!r} at {base} and is "
                    f"{now[name]!r} here; that cell is fixed when the row is written"
                )
            elif was[name] != UNRECORDED:
                problems.append(
                    f"{rel}:{line}: {name} was already recorded as {was[name]!r} at "
                    f"{base}; a pending cell is written once"
                )


def cmd_check(args):
    repo_root = args.repo_root
    log_path = args.log or str(pathlib.Path(repo_root) / "docs/log/task-outcomes.tsv")
    problems = []
    header, rows = read_log(log_path)
    if not check_shape(header, rows, log_path, problems):
        report(problems)
        return 1
    check_values(rows, log_path, problems)
    check_coverage(rows, repo_root, log_path, problems)
    if not args.no_base:
        check_append_only(rows, repo_root, log_path, args.base, problems)
    if problems:
        report(problems)
        return 1
    print(f"ok: {len(rows)} rows, {len(briefs(repo_root))} briefs, "
          f"schema {SCHEMA}")
    return 0


def report(problems):
    for p in problems:
        print(p, file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"\n{len(problems)} problem(s). docs/measurement.md is the authority "
          "on every rule above.", file=sys.stderr)


# --- derive --------------------------------------------------------------


def run_git(repo_root, *args):
    # TZ=UTC so that any `--date=` rendering git does resolves in UTC.
    # Schema 1 defines every date as UTC (docs/measurement.md), but git's
    # short/format renderings otherwise use each commit's own timezone,
    # which would put a date on the wrong calendar day for commits made
    # west of UTC late in the day. Forcing the environment is the only
    # rendering-independent fix. Task 0070 review, finding P1.
    env = {**os.environ, "TZ": "UTC"}
    proc = subprocess.run(["git", *args], cwd=repo_root, env=env,
                          capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
    return proc.stdout


def brief_added(repo_root, path):
    """The date a file with this number first appeared, following renames."""
    out = run_git(repo_root, "log", "--diff-filter=A", "--follow",
                  "--format=%H %ad", "--date=short", "--", str(path)).strip()
    if not out:
        return None, None
    commit, date = out.splitlines()[-1].split(" ", 1)
    return commit, date


PR_RE = re.compile(
    r"^Merge pull request #(?P<pr>\d+) from [^/]+/(?:[^/\s]+/)?(?P<num>\d{4})-")


def merges_by_task(repo_root, main):
    """Task number -> (date, pr) for every merge whose branch names it.

    A branch called `emcee/0067-…` is the strongest signal available for
    when a task's *work* landed, and it survives the brief being renamed
    on the way (0067's branch and its brief disagree about the slug).
    Earliest merge wins: that is the first landing, which is what a time
    series wants.
    """
    out = run_git(repo_root, "log", "--merges", "--format=%ad%x09%s",
                  "--date=short", main)
    found = {}
    for line in out.splitlines():
        date, _, subject = line.partition("\t")
        m = PR_RE.match(subject)
        if m:
            found[m.group("num")] = (date, m.group("pr"))
    return found  # git log is newest-first, so the last write is the earliest


def task_landing(repo_root, stem, have, by_num_brief, merges, main):
    """When this task's work landed, and on which pull request.

    The branch-name answer is preferred, but only where the number
    identifies one brief. This repository has two briefs numbered 0003,
    and a branch named after that number cannot say which of them it
    carried — writing one task's landing date into the other's row is
    exactly the kind of confident wrong number the log must not hold.
    """
    num = stem[:4]
    if len(by_num_brief.get(num, [])) == 1 and num in merges:
        return merges[num]
    path = have.get(stem)
    if path is None:
        return (None, None)
    commit, _ = brief_added(repo_root, path)
    if not commit:
        return (None, None)
    return landing(repo_root, commit, main)


ADD_PR_RE = re.compile(r"^Merge pull request #(\d+) ")


def landing(repo_root, commit, main):
    """When the commit that introduced a brief reached the trunk.

    The fallback, for the era before branches were named after task
    numbers. It answers a weaker question than `merges_by_task` — a
    brief queued as a prompt reaches the trunk on whatever pull request
    queued it, which may not be the one that did the work — so the
    caller prefers the branch-name answer and only falls back here.

    Work that has not reached the trunk has not landed, and this says so
    rather than dating the landing to the commit's own day. Without the
    ancestry test, running this on the branch that is *doing* the work
    marks the work merged — into `outcome` and `landed`, which are
    written once and never corrected.
    """
    reachable = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", commit, main],
        cwd=repo_root, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    if reachable.returncode != 0:
        return (None, None)
    out = run_git(repo_root, "rev-list", "--ancestry-path", "--merges",
                  f"{commit}..{main}").strip()
    if not out:
        # Landed straight on the trunk, as this repository's earliest work did.
        date = run_git(repo_root, "log", "-1", "--format=%ad", "--date=short",
                       commit).strip()
        return date, UNRECORDED
    merge = out.splitlines()[-1]
    date = run_git(repo_root, "log", "-1", "--format=%ad", "--date=short",
                   merge).strip()
    subject = run_git(repo_root, "log", "-1", "--format=%s", merge).strip()
    m = ADD_PR_RE.match(subject)
    return date, (m.group(1) if m else UNRECORDED)


HEADER_RE = re.compile(r"^([A-Za-z-]+):\s*(.*)$")


def brief_headers(path):
    """The `Key: value` header block, stopping where the parser stops."""
    headers = {}
    for line in path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        if not line.strip():
            break
        m = HEADER_RE.match(line)
        if not m:
            break
        headers[m.group(1).lower()] = m.group(2).strip()
    return headers


def normalise_milestone(value):
    """The `Milestone:` header as a bare key.

    Two shapes reach here: a clause key from docs/state/MILESTONE.md,
    written bare or in brackets, and the explicit opt-out `none —
    hygiene`. Anything else is left unrecorded rather than guessed at —
    a milestone the log invents is worse than one it admits to not
    knowing, because off-milestone share is computed from this column.
    """
    v = value.strip().strip("[]").lower()
    if not v:
        return UNRECORDED
    if v.startswith("none"):
        return "none-hygiene"
    if KEY_RE.match(v):
        return v
    return UNRECORDED


def harness_facts(paths):
    """Per-task cost, turns and model from a harness journal, if given.

    Numbers only. The journal is the operator's, lives outside this
    repository, and nothing textual from it is ever copied in.

    Two rules keep this from writing a number that means something other
    than it says, both into cells that are written once and never
    corrected:

    *A missing figure stays missing.* A `usage` event with no cost, or a
    null one, contributes nothing rather than `0.00` — a recorded zero
    claims the attempt was free.

    *An ambiguous figure stays missing too.* Several `usage` events for
    one task means the task was attempted more than once, and this
    derivation writes a single row per task. Summing would put the cost
    of several attempts in a column that means "this attempt", and
    picking one would be arbitrary, so the cell is left unrecorded and
    the task is named on stderr for whoever wants to split the attempts
    by hand.
    """
    seen = {}
    for p in paths:
        f = pathlib.Path(p)
        if not f.is_file():
            print(f"note: no harness journal at {p}", file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        for line in f.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
            try:
                ev = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            task = ev.get("task") or ev.get("step")
            if not task:
                continue
            slot = seen.setdefault(task, {"usage": [], "model": None, "pr": None})
            if ev.get("type") == "usage":
                slot["usage"].append(ev)
            elif ev.get("type") == "step_started" and ev.get("model"):
                slot["model"] = str(ev["model"])
            elif ev.get("type") == "pr" and ev.get("url"):
                slot["pr"] = str(ev["url"]).rstrip("/").rsplit("/", 1)[-1]

    facts = {}
    ambiguous = []
    for task, slot in seen.items():
        out = {}
        if slot["model"]:
            out["model"] = slot["model"]
        if slot["pr"]:
            out["pr"] = slot["pr"]
        if len(slot["usage"]) == 1:
            ev = slot["usage"][0]
            cost = ev.get("cost_usd")
            if isinstance(cost, (int, float)):
                out["cost_usd"] = f"{float(cost):.2f}"
            turns = ev.get("turns")
            if isinstance(turns, int):
                out["turns"] = str(turns)
        elif len(slot["usage"]) > 1:
            ambiguous.append(task)
        facts[task] = out
    if ambiguous:
        print("note: cost and turns left unrecorded for "
              f"{len(ambiguous)} task(s) attempted more than once: "
              + ", ".join(sorted(ambiguous)), file=sys.stderr)
    return facts


def blank_row():
    return {c: UNRECORDED for c in COLUMNS}


def cmd_derive(args):
    repo_root = args.repo_root
    log_path = args.log or str(pathlib.Path(repo_root) / "docs/log/task-outcomes.tsv")
    have = briefs(repo_root)
    facts = harness_facts(args.harness_journal)
    merges = merges_by_task(repo_root, args.main)
    by_num_brief = {}
    for stem in have:
        by_num_brief.setdefault(stem[:4], []).append(stem)
    _, existing_rows = read_log(log_path)
    known = {as_record(r)["task"] for r in existing_rows}
    # Numbers already logged, and how many times each is attempted.
    # A brief renamed after its row was written keeps its number but
    # changes its stem, so a stem-only check would append a second row
    # for the same task. Where the number denotes exactly one current
    # brief, a logged number means that brief is already covered under
    # its old stem. The two-0003 case in this repo's history is why the
    # uniqueness guard is there rather than a bare number match. Task
    # 0070 review, finding P1.
    known_nums = {as_record(r)["task"][:4] for r in existing_rows}
    attempts = collections.Counter(as_record(r)["task"] for r in existing_rows)

    new = []
    for stem, path in sorted(have.items()):
        if stem in known:
            continue
        num = stem[:4]
        if num in known_nums and len(by_num_brief.get(num, [])) == 1:
            continue
        _, queued = brief_added(repo_root, path)
        row = blank_row()
        row["schema"] = SCHEMA
        row["task"] = stem
        row["attempt"] = "1"
        row["queued"] = queued or UNRECORDED
        landed, pr = task_landing(repo_root, stem, have, by_num_brief,
                                  merges, args.main)
        if landed:
            row["landed"] = landed
            row["pr"] = pr or UNRECORDED
            row["outcome"] = "merged"
        headers = brief_headers(path)
        tier = headers.get("model", UNRECORDED).lower()
        row["tier"] = tier if tier in TIERS else UNRECORDED
        row["milestone"] = normalise_milestone(headers.get("milestone", ""))
        f = facts.get(stem, {})
        for name in ("cost_usd", "turns", "model", "pr"):
            if f.get(name):
                row[name] = f[name]
        row["env"] = args.env
        row["provenance"] = args.provenance
        new.append(row)

    if args.fill:
        out = [ "\t".join(COLUMNS) ]
        for r in existing_rows:
            rec = as_record(r)
            f = facts.get(rec["task"], {})
            # Harness facts are keyed by task, not by attempt, so they
            # are a per-task aggregate. Applying them to a task with more
            # than one attempt would attribute the whole task's spend to
            # each row — exactly what docs/measurement.md forbids ("Left
            # `-` where the ledger cannot say — a task attempted more
            # than once"). Only fill when there is a single attempt to
            # attribute to. Task 0070 review, finding P1.
            if attempts[rec["task"]] == 1:
                for name in ("cost_usd", "turns", "pr"):
                    if rec[name] == UNRECORDED and f.get(name):
                        rec[name] = f[name]
            if rec["outcome"] == UNRECORDED:
                landed, pr = task_landing(repo_root, rec["task"], have,
                                          by_num_brief, merges, args.main)
                if landed:
                    rec["outcome"] = "merged"
                    # Every one of these is write-once. A cell already
                    # carrying a value was written by someone who knew
                    # something this derivation does not.
                    if rec["landed"] == UNRECORDED:
                        rec["landed"] = landed
                    if rec["pr"] == UNRECORDED and pr:
                        rec["pr"] = pr
            out.append("\t".join(rec[c] for c in COLUMNS))
        for row in new:
            out.append("\t".join(row[c] for c in COLUMNS))
        text = "\n".join(out) + "\n"
        target = args.output or log_path
        pathlib.Path(target).write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
        print(f"wrote {target}: {len(existing_rows)} rows kept, {len(new)} appended",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 0

    for row in new:
        print("\t".join(row[c] for c in COLUMNS))
    if not new:
        print("every brief already has a row", file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


# --- redirect ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT THIS COMMAND IS, AND THE LINE IT MUST NOT CROSS
#
# `docs/architecture.md` Proposal 06 divides outcomes into receipts,
# which the system may author, and verdicts, which only the resident
# may. Columns 15 and 16 are verdicts. Nothing here authors one.
#
# What this does is *transcribe* a verdict the resident already stated,
# somewhere with an address. `--ref` is the proof of authorship, and it
# is the whole integrity story: an agent may be the resident's hand, and
# may never be a second opinion.
#
# Fabrication therefore has to be detectable rather than merely
# discouraged, and resolution alone is not detection — a `--ref` pointing
# at a real comment the *agent* wrote would satisfy an existence check
# while carrying no resident judgment at all. So two things are verified
# mechanically before a cell moves: the reference resolves to a real
# comment or record, and its author is the resident.
#
# THE RESIDUE, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN
#
# Two things no resolver can check, both of which belong to the weekly
# audit's sampled reads:
#
#   * Whether the cited text actually states the verdict claimed for it.
#     A resident comment exists; that it says "this redirect was wrong"
#     is a semantic claim, and a machine that judged it would be
#     authoring the verdict it was supposed to be transcribing.
#   * Whether the resident's *credential* was the resident's *hand*. An
#     agent holding the resident's forge token can post as them. The
#     author check raises the cost of fabrication and bounds who can do
#     it; it does not make it impossible.
#
# Both are said in the command's help text too, so nobody meets the
# mechanical check and mistakes it for the semantic one.

PR_REF_RE = re.compile(
    # The URL a resident would paste, and the form the log stores. Both,
    # because the stored form is what an agent replaying its own step
    # copies back out of the cell, and a replay that could not parse
    # what the tool itself wrote would be idempotent in theory only.
    r"^(?:https?://[^\s]*?/pull/|pr[-/])?(?P<pr>\d+)"
    r"[#/-](?:issue)?(?P<what>comment|pullrequestreview|review)-?(?P<id>\d+)$",
    re.IGNORECASE,
)
RECORD_REF_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:record-)?(?P<id>\d{8}T\d{6}Z-[a-z]+-[0-9a-f]+)$")

# The two record types agent/castle's single write choke point refuses to
# any worker tenant (`write_record`, task 0021 §2.4(e) and task 0023 §5).
# They are this journal's mechanical answer to "did the resident write
# this?" — a record carries no author field, but these two types cannot
# have come from a seat.
RESIDENT_RECORD_TYPES = ("answer", "correction")

RESIDENT_ENV = "OUTCOMES_RESIDENT"
# Write access is not enough to author a verdict — a bot, a CI token and
# a read-only collaborator can all hold it or be mistaken for it. The
# same reasoning as the review-findings workflow's permission check, one
# notch stricter because this writes into an append-only log.
RESIDENT_PERMISSIONS = ("admin", "maintain")


def gh(*args):
    """`gh api …`, or a clear refusal. No silent skip; see the header."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(["gh", *args], capture_output=True, text=True)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        raise Fail(
            "gh is not installed, so `--ref` cannot be resolved and its author "
            "cannot be checked. This command refuses to write an unverified "
            "verdict rather than offering a flag to skip the check."
        )
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise Fail(f"gh {' '.join(args)} failed: {proc.stderr.strip()}")
    return proc.stdout


def current_repo(args):
    if args.repo:
        return args.repo
    return gh("repo", "view", "--json", "nameWithOwner",
              "--jq", ".nameWithOwner").strip()


def parse_ref(raw):
    """A resident's citation, in any shape they might paste, normalised.

    Accepted: a full pull-request comment URL, the `pr-120-comment-99`
    form the log stores, and a bare journal record id. Everything else is
    refused rather than guessed at — a citation that points somewhere
    slightly wrong is worse than none, because it looks like evidence.
    """
    raw = raw.strip()
    m = RECORD_REF_RE.match(raw)
    if m:
        return ("record", None, m.group("id"), f"record-{m.group('id')}")
    m = PR_REF_RE.match(raw)
    if m:
        what = "review" if "review" in m.group("what").lower() else "comment"
        pr, ident = m.group("pr"), m.group("id")
        return ("pr", pr, ident, f"pr-{pr}-{what}-{ident}")
    raise Fail(
        f"--ref {raw!r} is not a citation this understands. Give a pull-request "
        "comment URL (…/pull/120#issuecomment-5659322373), the stored form "
        "(pr-120-comment-5659322373), or a journal record id "
        "(20260914T051123Z-answer-ab12cd)."
    )


def resident_login(args, repo, login):
    """Is `login` the resident? Configuration first, then the forge.

    Principle 01: the mechanism is public, the identity is private. The
    resident's forge login is *their* fact, so it is configuration —
    `--resident`, or OUTCOMES_RESIDENT — and this repository holds none
    of it. With nothing configured the question is asked of the forge
    instead, which answers it without anyone writing a name down: an
    account with admin or maintain on the repository is the resident of
    this installation for the purpose of authoring a verdict.
    """
    configured = args.resident or os.environ.get(RESIDENT_ENV, "").strip()
    if configured:
        if login.lower() != configured.lower():
            raise Fail(
                f"--ref was written by {login!r}, and the configured resident is "
                f"{configured!r}. A verdict is transcribed from the resident, "
                "never from anyone else — including from the agent doing the "
                "transcribing."
            )
        return
    out = gh("api", f"repos/{repo}/collaborators/{login}/permission",
             "--jq", ".permission").strip()
    if out not in RESIDENT_PERMISSIONS:
        raise Fail(
            f"--ref was written by {login!r}, whose permission on {repo} is "
            f"{out!r}; a verdict must come from an account with "
            f"{' or '.join(RESIDENT_PERMISSIONS)}. Set {RESIDENT_ENV} or pass "
            "--resident to name the resident explicitly instead."
        )


def verify_pr_ref(args, repo, pr, ident, stored):
    kind = "review" if "-review-" in stored else "comment"
    if kind == "comment":
        path = f"repos/{repo}/issues/comments/{ident}"
    else:
        path = f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/reviews/{ident}"
    raw = gh("api", path)
    try:
        payload = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        raise Fail(f"{path} did not return JSON")
    user = payload.get("user") or {}
    login = user.get("login") or ""
    if not login:
        raise Fail(f"--ref {stored} resolved to a {kind} with no author")
    if user.get("type") != "User":
        raise Fail(
            f"--ref {stored} was written by {login!r}, a {user.get('type')!r}. "
            "A verdict is the resident's; a bot account cannot hold one."
        )
    # A citation that resolves but belongs to a different pull request
    # would attribute the resident's words to the wrong attempt.
    where = payload.get("issue_url") or payload.get("pull_request_url") or ""
    if where and not where.rstrip("/").endswith(f"/{pr}"):
        raise Fail(
            f"--ref {stored} names pull request {pr} but the {kind} belongs to "
            f"{where.rstrip('/').rsplit('/', 1)[-1]}"
        )
    resident_login(args, repo, login)
    return login


def journal_dir(args):
    if args.journal_dir:
        return pathlib.Path(args.journal_dir)
    env = os.environ.get("CASTLE_STATE_DIR")
    if env:
        return pathlib.Path(env) / "journal"
    xdg = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME")
    if xdg:
        return pathlib.Path(xdg) / "castle" / "journal"
    return pathlib.Path.home() / ".local" / "state" / "castle" / "journal"


def verify_record_ref(args, ident, stored):
    """A journal record id, checked against the journal it names.

    The journal is private-layer data and lives outside this repository;
    only the record's *id* and *type* are read, and nothing textual from
    it is ever copied in (docs/measurement.md's rule for the whole log).
    """
    d = journal_dir(args)
    path = d / f"{ident}.md"
    if not path.is_file():
        raise Fail(
            f"--ref {stored} names no record at {path}. Point --journal-dir at "
            "the journal that holds it, or set CASTLE_STATE_DIR."
        )
    fields = {}
    for line in path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()[1:]:
        if line.strip() == "---":
            break
        if ":" in line:
            k, _, v = line.partition(":")
            fields[k.strip()] = v.strip()
    rtype = fields.get("type", "")
    if rtype not in RESIDENT_RECORD_TYPES:
        raise Fail(
            f"--ref {stored} is a {rtype!r} record. Only "
            f"{' and '.join(RESIDENT_RECORD_TYPES)} records are refused to every "
            "worker tenant by agent/castle's write choke point, so they are the "
            "only record types that prove the resident wrote them."
        )
    return rtype


def find_row(rows, task, attempt):
    """The row a task names, and a refusal rather than a guess if unsure."""
    by_task = {}
    for row in rows:
        rec = as_record(row)
        by_task.setdefault(rec["task"], []).append(row)
    if task not in by_task:
        nums = sorted({s for s in by_task if s[:4] == task[:4]})
        if len(nums) == 1:
            task = nums[0]
        elif len(nums) > 1:
            raise Fail(f"{task} matches more than one logged task: "
                       + ", ".join(nums))
        else:
            raise Fail(
                f"no row for {task}. A verdict matures a row that already "
                "exists; the row is written by `derive` when the attempt starts."
            )
    candidates = by_task[task]
    if attempt is not None:
        for row in candidates:
            if as_record(row)["attempt"] == str(attempt):
                return row
        raise Fail(f"{task} has no attempt {attempt}")
    if len(candidates) > 1:
        # Guessing which attempt the resident meant would put their words
        # on the wrong row, permanently.
        raise Fail(
            f"{task} has {len(candidates)} attempts; say which with --attempt"
        )
    return candidates[0]


def cmd_redirect(args):
    repo_root = args.repo_root
    log_path = args.log or str(pathlib.Path(repo_root) / "docs/log/task-outcomes.tsv")
    header, rows = read_log(log_path)
    if header != COLUMNS:
        raise Fail(f"{log_path}: header is not schema {SCHEMA}'s columns")

    # Every row is about to be rebuilt from `COLUMNS`, and `as_record`
    # would quietly drop cells past the end or pad a short row with
    # nothing. On an append-only log that is the one class of damage
    # there is no way back from, so a malformed row stops the write
    # rather than being normalised by it.
    for r in rows:
        if len(r["_cells"]) != len(COLUMNS):
            raise Fail(
                f"{log_path}:{r['_line']}: {len(r['_cells'])} cells, expected "
                f"{len(COLUMNS)}; run `outcomes check` and repair the log before "
                "writing a verdict into it"
            )

    row = find_row(rows, args.task, args.attempt)
    rec = as_record(row)
    if rec["probe"] != UNRECORDED:
        raise Fail(f"{rec['task']} is a probe row; salt carries no verdict")

    if args.wrong is not None and args.wrong < 0:
        raise Fail("--wrong counts redirects judged wrong; it cannot be negative")
    kind = "reassessed" if args.wrong is not None else "redirect"
    ref_kind, pr, ident, body = parse_ref(args.ref)
    citation = f"{kind}/{body}"
    cites, problems = parse_citations(rec["verdict_ref"])
    if problems:
        raise Fail("; ".join(problems))
    existing = [c[2] for c in cites]

    # Replay first, and without the network. A citation already in the
    # cell was verified when it was recorded; re-resolving it would buy
    # no new guarantee and would make an idempotent retry fail whenever
    # the forge is unreachable. "Exit zero, change nothing" is the
    # contract an agent re-running its own step depends on.
    if citation in existing:
        if kind == "reassessed" and rec["redirects_wrong"] != str(args.wrong):
            raise Fail(
                f"{citation} already licensed redirects_wrong="
                f"{rec['redirects_wrong']}; changing it to {args.wrong} needs its "
                "own citation to wherever the resident said the new number"
            )
        print(f"{rec['task']} attempt {rec['attempt']}: {citation} is already "
              f"recorded; nothing changed (redirects={rec['redirects']}, "
              f"redirects_wrong={rec['redirects_wrong']})")
        return 0

    if ref_kind == "pr":
        repo = current_repo(args)
        who = verify_pr_ref(args, repo, pr, ident, body)
        proof = f"{body} on {repo}, written by {who}"
    else:
        rtype = verify_record_ref(args, ident, body)
        proof = f"{body}, a {rtype} record"

    n_redirect = sum(1 for c in cites if c[0] == "redirect")
    if kind == "redirect":
        rec["redirects"] = str(n_redirect + 1)
    else:
        if rec["redirects"] == UNRECORDED:
            raise Fail(
                f"{rec['task']} has no recorded redirect to reassess. Log the "
                "redirect itself first, citing where the resident made it."
            )
        if args.wrong > int(rec["redirects"]):
            raise Fail(
                f"--wrong {args.wrong} exceeds the {rec['redirects']} redirect(s) "
                "recorded for this attempt"
            )
        rec["redirects_wrong"] = str(args.wrong)
    rec["verdict_ref"] = ",".join(existing + [citation])

    # Build the whole file, then hold it to `check`'s value rules before
    # anything is written. A log that only fails CI after the fact is a
    # log someone has to go and repair by hand, and repairing an
    # append-only log is the thing it exists to prevent.
    out = ["\t".join(COLUMNS)]
    for r in rows:
        out.append("\t".join((rec if r is row else as_record(r))[c] for c in COLUMNS))
    text = "\n".join(out) + "\n"

    staged = []
    for n, line in enumerate(text.split("\n")[:-1][1:], start=2):
        staged.append({"_line": n, "_cells": line.split("\t")})
    problems = []
    check_values(staged, log_path, problems)
    if problems:
        report(problems)
        raise Fail("refusing to write; the result would not pass `outcomes check`")

    pathlib.Path(log_path).write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    print(f"{rec['task']} attempt {rec['attempt']}: recorded {citation}")
    print(f"  transcribed from {proof}")
    print(f"  redirects={rec['redirects']} redirects_wrong={rec['redirects_wrong']}"
          + ("  (pending: no reassessment yet)"
             if rec["redirects_wrong"] == UNRECORDED else ""))
    print("  commit this with the work; `outcomes check` holds it against the trunk")
    return 0


REDIRECT_HELP = """\
Transcribe a redirect the resident made, or their later reassessment of
one, into a task's row.

  outcomes redirect <task> --ref <citation>            a redirect happened
  outcomes redirect <task> --ref <citation> --wrong N  the resident reassessed

`redirects` is the number of *distinct cited* redirect events. Every
invocation needs --ref. A --ref already recorded is an idempotent replay:
exit zero, change nothing. A new --ref adds a citation and moves the
count. The count never moves any other way, and no cell is ever edited.

`redirects_wrong` stays `-` — pending — until the resident reassesses.
It is never defaulted to 0. The measure it feeds is defined over
redirects *later judged* wrong, so a 0 written at redirect time would
record the absence of a judgment as a judgment of correctness and bias
the conditional miscorrection rate downward. Pending is the truthful
value, and `-` beside a recorded `redirects` says exactly that.

WHAT THIS COMMAND IS NOT

It does not author a verdict. Proposal 06 (docs/architecture.md) says no
automation may, and this does not: it transcribes a verdict the resident
stated somewhere with an address, and --ref is the proof of authorship.
An agent may be the resident's hand. It may never be a second opinion,
and it may never invent a redirect.

Two things are checked mechanically before any cell moves: the reference
resolves to a real comment or record, and its author is the resident —
the forge login for a pull-request reference, and for a journal record
id, a record type that agent/castle refuses to write on any tenant's
behalf.

Two things are not, and cannot be:

  * Whether the cited text actually states the verdict claimed for it.
    That is a semantic reading, and a machine that did it would be
    authoring the verdict it is meant to be transcribing. It belongs to
    the weekly audit's sampled reads.
  * Whether the resident's credential was the resident's hand. An agent
    holding the resident's forge token can post as them. This check
    bounds who can fabricate; it does not make fabrication impossible.

Resolution is not detection, and passing this check is not the same as
being true. Say so wherever the numbers are reported.
"""


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split("\n")[0])
    ap.add_argument("--repo-root", default=".",
                    help="repository to read (default: the current directory)")
    ap.add_argument("--log", help="the log file (default: docs/log/task-outcomes.tsv)")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    c = sub.add_parser("check", help="verify the log; no network, no model")
    c.add_argument("--base", default="origin/main",
                   help="revision to hold the log's history against "
                        "(default: origin/main; CI passes the merge base "
                        "with it, never a local branch)")
    c.add_argument("--no-base", action="store_true",
                   help="skip the append-only comparison (a fresh clone with no remote)")
    c.set_defaults(func=cmd_check)

    d = sub.add_parser("derive", help="write rows from artifact state")
    d.add_argument("--harness-journal", action="append", default=[],
                   help="a harness journal to take cost and turns from; repeatable")
    d.add_argument("--env", default=UNRECORDED,
                   help="the environment key for new rows (docs/measurement.md)")
    d.add_argument("--provenance", default="live", choices=sorted(PROVENANCE))
    d.add_argument("--main", default="origin/main",
                   help="the trunk to measure landing against")
    d.add_argument("--fill", action="store_true",
                   help="rewrite the log, filling pending cells")
    d.add_argument("-o", "--output", help="write somewhere other than the log")
    d.set_defaults(func=cmd_derive)

    r = sub.add_parser(
        "redirect",
        help="transcribe a resident redirect, or their reassessment of one",
        description=REDIRECT_HELP,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
    )
    r.add_argument("task", help="the brief's stem, or its number if unambiguous")
    r.add_argument("--ref", required=True,
                   help="where the resident said it: a pull-request comment URL, "
                        "pr-N-comment-N, or a journal record id")
    r.add_argument("--wrong", type=int, metavar="N",
                   help="transcribe a reassessment: how many of this attempt's "
                        "redirects the resident has now judged wrong. Omit it and "
                        "the cell stays pending, which is what it should be until "
                        "they have looked again.")
    r.add_argument("--attempt", type=int,
                   help="which attempt, where a task has more than one row")
    r.add_argument("--resident", metavar="LOGIN",
                   help=f"the resident's forge login (or ${RESIDENT_ENV}). With "
                        "neither, an account with admin or maintain on the "
                        "repository is taken to be the resident.")
    r.add_argument("--repo", metavar="OWNER/NAME",
                   help="the repository a pull-request reference belongs to "
                        "(default: whatever `gh repo view` resolves here)")
    r.add_argument("--journal-dir", metavar="DIR",
                   help="the journal a record id names (default: "
                        "$CASTLE_STATE_DIR/journal)")
    r.set_defaults(func=cmd_redirect)

    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if getattr(args, "provenance", None) == "live" and args.env == UNRECORDED:
        # `env` is immutable, so a live row written without one can never
        # gain it. That is the single irreversible way to break the
        # pooling requirement docs/measurement.md claims is met by
        # construction, so it is refused rather than warned about.
        ap.error("--env is required for a live row; see the environment "
                 "table in docs/measurement.md")
    try:
        return args.func(args)
    except Fail as e:
        print(f"error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
